  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the maximum magnitude outcome (highest absolute value) of each lottery and prefers the one with the more favorable (i.e., less negative) such outcome, weighted by its probability. In this case, the maximum magnitude outcomes are -5000 (B) and -5 (A). The individual’s verbal report highlights the disproportionate nature of the loss in B relative to A, which aligns with focusing on the maximum magnitude outcome, particularly the large negative one in B. Although the individual does not explicitly mention probabilities or weighting, the emphasis on the extreme loss in B suggests that the large magnitude of the worst outcome played a decisive role in their reasoning. This is consistent with the core idea of the decision reason—evaluating the most extreme (in magnitude) outcome and being sensitive to its severity. While the weighting by probability is not explicitly discussed, the contrast drawn ('in no proportion') may implicitly reflect a judgment that such a large loss, even if rare, is unacceptable compared to a trivial certain loss. Therefore, the information considered (maximum magnitude loss) and the justification (disproportionate loss) align closely with the decision reason, though the probabilistic weighting is not clearly articulated. The match is strong but not perfect.",
